        <!-- Article Body -->
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A rectifier does not draw current as a sine wave. It draws it in short, steep pulses near the voltage peak, and the Fourier series of that pulse train is the fundamental plus the 5th, 7th, 11th, 13th and higher orders. Those orders flow back toward the source as harmonic current, heat transformers and cables, and distort the voltage every other load on the bus sees. This article covers how the distortion is measured, what it costs, why fixed filters fall short, and how a <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">CHITEK active harmonic filter</strong> cancels it at the point of origin.</p>
<h2 id="how-harmonic-distortion-is-quantified" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">How Harmonic Distortion Is Quantified</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">The standard metric is total demand distortion at the point of common coupling (PCC):</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">THDi% = √(Σ I_h², h = 2…50) / I_L × 100%</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6"><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">IEEE 519</strong> sets the limit by short-circuit ratio (I_SC/I_L) and by load size — a typical <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">C&amp;I</strong> bus with I_SC/I_L below 20 is held to roughly <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">5% TDD</strong>. IEC 61000-3-2 and 61000-3-12 govern equipment emission; GB/T 14549 covers the Chinese grid equivalent. Measure current with a class-A analyzer over a full duty cycle, not a single snapshot.</p>
<h2 id="where-the-distortion-comes-from" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Where the Distortion Comes From</h2>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Six-pulse rectifiers</strong> — the dominant source in drives, UPS and DC chargers; characteristic orders are <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">5, 7, 11, 13</strong> at roughly 1/h of fundamental.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Single-phase switch-mode supplies</strong> — LED lighting, IT loads and office equipment inject <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">3rd and triplen</strong> orders that accumulate in the neutral.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Arc and induction furnaces</strong> — broadband, rapidly varying distortion plus reactive swing.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Regenerative drives and elevators</strong> — active front ends switch at high frequency and add interharmonic content.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Capacitor banks</strong> — not a source, but an amplifier: they shift the resonance point down toward a characteristic order.</li></ul>
<h2 id="what-it-costs" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">What It Costs</h2>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Transformer heating</strong> — eddy and stray losses rise with the square of frequency; a K-rated unit derates where a standard one fails.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Cable and neutral overload</strong> — triplen harmonics sum in the neutral and can exceed phase current.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Motor and generator stress</strong> — negative-sequence and harmonic fields add rotor heating beyond nameplate assumptions.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Capacitor failure</strong> — dielectric loss and resonance current shorten bank life and can rupture cans.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Power factor penalties</strong> — distortion power depresses true PF, so the utility bills for kVA the plant never used.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Protection misoperation</strong> — distorted zero-sequence current trips residual relays and nuisance breakers.</li></ul>
<h2 id="why-fixed-methods-fall-short" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Why Fixed Methods Fall Short</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Detuned reactors and passive filters are tuned to one order at a fixed impedance. They work only at design load, they can resonate with the source impedance when the network changes, and they cannot follow a load that swings through the day. A switched capacitor bank corrects average displacement PF but cannot cancel current harmonics or rebalance per phase. A <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">static var generator</strong> handles the reactive and balancing side dynamically; pairing it with an <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">active harmonic filter</strong> covers the waveform side. Neither fixed technology can do both as the load moves.</p>
<h2 id="how-an-ahf-cancels-harmonics-at-source" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">How an AHF Cancels Harmonics at Source</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">The <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">CHITEK AHF</strong> is a voltage-source converter that measures load current and synthesizes the inverse of its harmonic content:</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">i_c* = i_L - i_s(fundamental)</p>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">An IGBT or SiC bridge switching at <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">15-20 kHz</strong> tracks that reference within <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">&lt; 1 ms</strong> and drives <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">THDi from 30%+ down to &lt; 5%</strong>, with selective orders cancelled up to the <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">50th</strong>. Because the correction is closed-loop, it follows the load instead of assuming it. That is the practical meaning of "at source": the counter-current is injected at the offending feeder, so harmonics never propagate to the transformer.</p>
<h2 id="where-the-svg-fits" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Where the SVG Fits</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">A <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">CHITEK SVG</strong> injects or absorbs reactive current per phase, independently, to hold voltage and power factor near unity: sub-cycle response, continuous <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">±1%</strong> regulation, <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">PF at 0.99+</strong>. It cancels the negative-sequence component a fixed bank cannot touch and carries the reactive burden so the AHF can concentrate on waveform correction. In a combined LV AHF + SVG platform, one controller coordinates both — no fixed tuning, no resonance risk, adaptive phase balancing as the load varies.</p>
<h2 id="sizing-a-worked-example" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Sizing: A Worked Example</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Assume a 1000 kVA transformer, 400 V, feeding a 600 A nonlinear load with measured <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">THDi of 28%</strong> and displacement PF of 0.86.</p>
<ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li>Harmonic current: 600 × 0.28 ≈ <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">168 A RMS</strong> of distortion to cancel.</li><li>Required filter rating: 168 A ÷ derating factor (typically 0.8–1.0 by order mix) ≈ <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">170–210 A</strong> — select the next standard size.</li><li>Reactive demand: 600 × √(1 − 0.86²) ≈ <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">306 kvar</strong> — size the SVG to that, plus headroom for swings.</li><li>Add <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">15-25%</strong> margin for future single-phase and EV load.</li></ol>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">State the assumptions; a filter sized to nameplate load rather than measured current is the most common specification error.</p>
<h2 id="igbt-vs-sic" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">IGBT vs SiC</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Conventional LV AHF and SVG units use IGBTs capped near <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">150 °C</strong>, switching typically below <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">20 kHz</strong>. SiC MOSFETs run at <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">200 °C</strong> junction, carry roughly <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">10×</strong> the breakdown field, switch at <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">2-3×</strong> the frequency and cut switching loss by about <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">50%</strong>. The higher switching frequency widens control bandwidth, shrinks the output filter and cabinet, and improves cancellation of high-order and interharmonic content. That is the direction the <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">CHITEK active harmonic filter</strong> and SVG platform is built on.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6"><table class="w-full border-collapse"><thead><tr class="bg-gray-50"><th class="text-left p-4 font-semibold text-text-dark border-b border-border-light">Aspect</th><th class="text-left p-4 font-semibold text-text-dark border-b border-border-light">IGBT</th><th class="text-left p-4 font-semibold text-text-dark border-b border-border-light">SiC MOSFET</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Junction temperature</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">~150 °C</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">~200 °C</td></tr><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Switching frequency</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">&lt; 20 kHz</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">2-3× higher</td></tr><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Switching loss</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">baseline</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">~50% lower</td></tr><tr><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">Cabinet size</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">larger</td><td class="p-4 border-b border-border-light text-text-mid font-medium">smaller</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="engineering-deployment-checklist" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Engineering Deployment Checklist</h2>
<ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li>Measure phase voltages and currents at the PCC over a full duty cycle; log THDi, TDD, PF and unbalance.</li><li>Place the AHF/SVG at the offending feeder, close to the nonlinear load, not at the main incomer.</li><li>Audit existing capacitor banks for resonance and mistuning before commissioning the active unit.</li><li>Check weak-grid margin and size the converter for the worst-case voltage dip.</li><li>Verify after commissioning: THDi against the <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">IEEE 519</strong> limit at the PCC, PF at <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">0.99+</strong>, and no relay misoperation.</li><li>Leave <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">15-25%</strong> headroom for added single-phase load.</li></ol>
<h2 id="typical-application-profiles" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Typical Application Profiles</h2>
<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mb-6 text-text-mid leading-relaxed"><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">EV charging hubs</strong> — single-phase charger clusters unbalance the bus; the SVG rebalances per phase while the AHF cancels rectifier harmonics.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Arc-furnace and steel plants</strong> — violent, unbalanced reactive swings; AHF + SVG stabilize voltage and waveform together.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">C&amp;I plants with mixed single/three-phase load</strong> — a combined unit cuts heating, neutral overload and PF penalties.</li><li><strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">Data centres and UPS rooms</strong> — high-order and interharmonic content from switch-mode supplies, corrected at the distribution board.</li></ul>
<h2 id="bottom-line" class="text-2xl font-bold font-[family-name:var(--font-barlow-condensed)] text-text-dark mt-10 mb-4">Bottom Line</h2>
<p class="text-text-mid text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">Harmonic distortion is a source problem: it originates at the rectifier and propagates outward, so it is best cancelled where it starts, adaptively, with a closed-loop converter. A low-voltage <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">active harmonic filter</strong> paired with a <strong class="font-semibold text-text-dark">static var generator</strong> restores waveform quality and balance without fixed tuning or resonance risk. CHITEK's LV AHF + SVG platforms are specified from exactly this measurement-first discipline. What is your measured THDi at the PCC — and which feeder is carrying it?</p>
